

W(1)                UNIX Programmer's Manual                   W(1)



NAME
     w - who is on and what they are doing

SYNOPSIS
     w [ -hlsuw ] [ user ]

DESCRIPTION
     _W prints a summary of the current activity on the system,
     beginning with a header line showing the current time, how
     long the system has been up, and the number of users logged
     in.  The following entries are then printed for each user:
     login name, the terminal name, the time the user logged on,
     and the command line of the most recent process on that
     terminal.

     _U_p_t_i_m_e is the same program under another name: invoked as
     _u_p_t_i_m_e (or with -u) it prints the header line and stops.

     The following options may be used:

     -h   Suppress the heading.

     -l   Give the long form output.  This is the default.

     -s   Give a short form output.  The terminal name is
          abbreviated and the login time is omitted.

     -u   Print the heading line only, as _u_p_t_i_m_e does.

     -w   Print the full listing, as _w does.

     If a _u_s_e_r name is given, output is restricted to that user.

FILES
     /etc/utmp     the login accounting file

SEE ALSO
     who(1), ps(1), utmp(5)

BUGS
     There are no idle, JCPU or PCPU columns, and the heading
     carries no load average, because this system measures none
     of those.  A terminal's last-input time is not recorded, no
     per-process CPU time is kept, and the scheduler holds no
     run-queue average.  Columns of zeroes would read as real
     measurements, so they are omitted rather than filled in.

     The most recent process on a terminal is chosen by process
     number alone.  Upstream prefers a process that is not
     ignoring interrupts, which needs a signal-state field the
     process record does not carry, so a backgrounded job can be
     reported where its shell would be more useful.

     A login name longer than eight characters is truncated in
     the accounting record and therefore here; see utmp(5).

FGX                            2.11BSD                           1
